<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
	Auto-update (2026-09-15) - exposes only the "Mine-imator/updates/" subfolder of the app's
	own internal files dir as a content:// URI the package installer can read the downloaded
	APK from. Nothing else under that directory is exposed.

	Fixed 2026-09-16 (KNOWN_ISSUES.md) - this originally declared path="updates/" (bare), on
	the wrong assumption that QDir::homePath() (what Context.getFilesDir() resolves to on
	Android) is where the app writes things directly. It isn't: user_directory_get()
	(UtilFunc.cpp) returns homePath() + "/Mine-imator/" - the SAME base dir as log.txt/
	Projects/, confirmed via `run-as` all session - so the real downloaded file lives at
	files/Mine-imator/updates/update.apk, one level deeper than this file declared.
	FileProvider.getUriForFile() requires the target to be under a declared root or it throws
	IllegalArgumentException - silently swallowed by android_install_apk()'s JniExceptionCleaner
	(UpdateFunc.cpp), which is exactly why the very first real end-to-end auto-update test
	failed with "No se pudo abrir el instalador" and nothing more specific in the log.
-->
<paths xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">
	<files-path name="updates" path="Mine-imator/updates/"/>

	<!-- "Compartir registro" (2026-09-16, user request - a friend testing on their own device
		needs a way to get log.txt out, since Android's scoped storage makes the app's internal
		folder invisible to any file manager, by OS design, regardless of permissions - there's
		no "enable" switch for that, the real fix is sharing the file directly).

		FIXED 2026-09-16, same day - the button "no responde" (user report). The first version
		of this entry declared path="Mine-imator/log.txt" (the file itself, not a directory), on
		the theory that FileProvider's canonical-path prefix check would treat a path matching
		itself as "inside" itself. Unverifiable without a device and apparently wrong in
		practice - FileProvider's own docs and every real-world example declare a DIRECTORY,
		never a bare filename, and getUriForFile() most likely throws IllegalArgumentException
		for a target that isn't strictly BELOW the declared root (silently swallowed by
		JniExceptionCleaner in android_share_log(), same failure shape as the original B35 bug -
		see that fix in UpdateFunc.cpp for why this class of failure is silent by default).
		Switched to the whole Mine-imator/ directory, the same convention already proven working
		for "updates" above. This exposes more than strictly needed (Projects/, settings.midata,
		etc. could ALSO get a content:// URI built for them), but nothing does that unless app
		code explicitly asks FileProvider for it - android_share_log() only ever requests
		log_file specifically, so nothing else actually becomes reachable in practice. -->
	<files-path name="logs" path="Mine-imator/"/>
</paths>
